    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Math has been shown to be a strong predictor of later academic and career achievement. However, there exists a gender gap in math achievement. Around the time girls enter middle school, both their achievement and engagement in math declines. Eventually, girls begin to opt out of higher-level math in high school, and thus, hindering opportunities to pursue certain technical fields such as engineering and computer science.\r\n \r\nMany factors affect young girls’ engagement in math. As math concepts become more abstract, it becomes harder to connect concepts with computations. Instructional practices that focus on memorization and speed computation alienate learners that are already struggling. Additionally, girls suffer from math anxiety that is aggravated by various social factors, such as lack of female role models, parental pressures, and competition from male classmates.\r\n \r\nIn order to regain confidence in their ability to do math and continue to pursue it, adolescent girls need to rediscover their passion for the subject. MathBuddy is a social game where a young girl interacts with a female avatar also learning math whose ability is slightly lower than the learner’s. Through guided activities, the learner tutors the avatar in math. In doing so, the learner engages higher-level cognitive skills such as analysis and evaluation. By teaching the avatar, the learner activates the protégé effect and becomes more engaged with math and reinforces her own math learning.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
